WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.417 --> 00:00:06.503
It should be no surprise

00:00:06.503 --> 00:00:10.215
that every community should have
a right to a clean environment.

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:13.635
Yet, some are sacrifice zones.

00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:14.970
Sacrifice zones:

00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:19.766
communities living on the front lines
of pollution and environmental hazards.

00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:23.853
But the good news is that we have
a unique opportunity

00:00:23.895 --> 00:00:25.480
to address legacy pollution

00:00:25.522 --> 00:00:30.735
as we together build an equitable
and just climate future.

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:34.364
Now, this is a story
about communities in crisis.

00:00:34.406 --> 00:00:38.702
Mostly, these are communities of Black
and brown and Indigenous peoples.

00:00:38.743 --> 00:00:42.455
It's often a story
of low-income communities.

00:00:42.497 --> 00:00:46.334
But race, race is the decisive factor.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.464
Now, studies show that an average
middle-income Black family

00:00:51.464 --> 00:00:55.135
with an 87,500-dollar income

00:00:55.135 --> 00:00:58.305
is likely to live with more pollution

00:00:58.346 --> 00:01:03.018
than a white family
making 22,500 dollars a year.

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:06.813
Now, my organization,
WE ACT for Environmental Justice,

00:01:06.855 --> 00:01:11.818
works within a movement of hundreds
of environmental justice groups,

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.236
here and abroad,

00:01:13.278 --> 00:01:18.450
to address the disproportionate impact
of pollution borne by our communities.

00:01:19.242 --> 00:01:22.412
So I'm talking
about environmental justice,

00:01:22.412 --> 00:01:26.082
which is a civil rights
and a human rights analysis

00:01:26.124 --> 00:01:27.792
of environmental decision making,

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.754
with a focus on the permitting,

00:01:30.795 --> 00:01:35.759
the permitting process that gives
polluters permission to pollute

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.597
within a regulatory standard
for air, water and soil.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.474
Now, these permits,

00:01:42.515 --> 00:01:47.020
they are an allowance that sacrifices
the health of community residents.

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:51.983
The cumulative effect of multiple
facilities sited in a community

00:01:51.983 --> 00:01:57.030
that emit high levels of pollution
in close proximity to where people live.

00:01:57.614 --> 00:02:00.867
That contributes to glaring
health disparities.

00:02:01.242 --> 00:02:04.371
Now, Harvard University studies
among Black Americans

00:02:04.412 --> 00:02:08.792
living in areas like Harlem
and the South Bronx,

00:02:08.833 --> 00:02:11.961
those are communities
which do not need clean air standards

00:02:12.003 --> 00:02:14.923
set by the Environmental
Protection Agency.

00:02:14.923 --> 00:02:16.966
And those studies that found

00:02:16.966 --> 00:02:20.303
that Black Americans have died of COVID-19

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:22.305
at higher rates than others

00:02:22.347 --> 00:02:25.392
due to living in air-polluted communities.

00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:27.686
Now, that's not a surprise

00:02:27.686 --> 00:02:30.689
since the majority of people
who live in areas

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.982
that do not meet clean air standards

00:02:33.024 --> 00:02:35.151
are Latinos and Black Americans.

00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:39.906
And that's the case in an area
called "Cancer Alley."

00:02:40.532 --> 00:02:44.077
Now, there's a song
sung by the late Nat King Cole

00:02:44.119 --> 00:02:45.787
called "Unforgettable."

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:48.790
And that is what "Cancer Alley" is.

00:02:48.832 --> 00:02:50.458
It is unforgettable.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.419
It's in the worst possible way.

00:02:52.919 --> 00:02:58.258
It's a 75-mile corridor between
New Orleans and Baton Rouge.

00:02:58.299 --> 00:03:04.055
It's a continuum of petrochemical
and plastics manufacturing facilities

00:03:04.097 --> 00:03:06.933
on acres of former plantations.

00:03:07.392 --> 00:03:09.602
Now, these facilities have created

00:03:09.644 --> 00:03:13.773
an intergenerational history
of death from cancer,

00:03:13.773 --> 00:03:16.901
with some communities
suffering cancer rates

00:03:16.943 --> 00:03:19.112
higher than the national rate.

00:03:20.572 --> 00:03:23.616
Now communities experience
environmental hazards

00:03:23.616 --> 00:03:26.453
and pollution exposure in diverse ways.

00:03:26.453 --> 00:03:27.704
In urban areas,

00:03:27.704 --> 00:03:31.750
mobile sources, contaminated sites,
they're really the challenge.

00:03:31.791 --> 00:03:37.297
And local governments generally manage
the infrastructure of pollution.

00:03:38.006 --> 00:03:42.677
But in smaller cities and rural areas,
industrial and oil refineries,

00:03:42.677 --> 00:03:46.222
landfills and incinerators,
they're usually the problem.

00:03:46.264 --> 00:03:49.017
And in places like Texas and California,

00:03:49.058 --> 00:03:51.728
there may be no zoning laws

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:56.816
that separate industrial facilities
from residential backyards.

00:03:57.442 --> 00:03:59.736
So environmental racism and injustice

00:03:59.736 --> 00:04:04.449
results from a complex legacy
of housing segregation,

00:04:04.449 --> 00:04:06.868
land use and zoning discrimination,

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:10.830
and from unequal enforcement and policies.

00:04:10.872 --> 00:04:13.333
Now decades ago,

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:15.335
policies such as redlining

00:04:15.335 --> 00:04:19.714
denied home loans to people of color
into certain communities.

00:04:20.006 --> 00:04:24.844
And this government policy
reinforced racial segregation in cities

00:04:24.886 --> 00:04:28.723
and diverted investments
away from those communities,

00:04:28.765 --> 00:04:32.227
creating large disparities
in home ownership

00:04:32.268 --> 00:04:35.355
as well as urban heat environments.

00:04:35.396 --> 00:04:38.399
A few trees and no open space.

00:04:38.441 --> 00:04:43.863
So today we're still living out
the legacy of those racist policies.

00:04:44.572 --> 00:04:48.034
Now, I first began organizing
around these issues

00:04:48.076 --> 00:04:52.956
as an elected Democratic district leader
in my West Harlem neighborhood.

00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:57.710
In 1988, I co-founded West Harlem
Environmental Action,

00:04:57.752 --> 00:05:00.547
known as WE ACT for Environmental Justice.

00:05:00.588 --> 00:05:02.924
And, you know, we began organizing,

00:05:02.966 --> 00:05:06.594
educating our neighbors
to understand the impact

00:05:06.636 --> 00:05:08.596
of the disproportionate siting

00:05:08.638 --> 00:05:11.850
and permitting of polluting facilities
in our communities.

00:05:12.433 --> 00:05:16.271
We started out by pressing New York City

00:05:16.271 --> 00:05:19.232
to fix the North River
sewage treatment plant

00:05:19.274 --> 00:05:20.441
in the Hudson River

00:05:20.441 --> 00:05:24.571
that was ruining our quality of life
by emitting toxic fumes.

00:05:24.571 --> 00:05:27.699
And we began to ask ourselves
a number of questions.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.495
How could we transform
the New York City diesel bus fleet

00:05:32.537 --> 00:05:34.289
to clean fuel buses

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:40.837
since we housed over 740 of the city
diesel bus fleet in uptown depots?

00:05:41.254 --> 00:05:43.673
How could we have a waterfront park

00:05:43.673 --> 00:05:47.385
along the beautiful Hudson River
instead of a parking lot?

00:05:47.927 --> 00:05:53.391
And how could we get environmental justice
on the agenda of New York City,

00:05:53.433 --> 00:05:57.937
the state and the federal government
to invest in sustainability

00:05:57.937 --> 00:05:59.355
in our communities?

00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:06.279
I know that it only takes one person
to reimagine what's possible,

00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:09.908
and some policy change takes decades.

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:11.910
It took us 18 years

00:06:11.951 --> 00:06:15.788
to transform the New York City
diesel bus fleet to hybrids.

00:06:15.788 --> 00:06:18.041
However, it took only a few months

00:06:18.082 --> 00:06:23.588
to ensure that all New York schools
are tested for lead in drinking water.

00:06:24.631 --> 00:06:30.136
I know that so much is possible
when we have the right political moment.

00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:31.930
But to capitalize,

00:06:31.971 --> 00:06:36.809
we must mobilize a critical mass of people
to create real change.

00:06:36.851 --> 00:06:40.188
And to monitor that our
policies are implemented

00:06:40.229 --> 00:06:42.065
in the way that was intended.

00:06:42.106 --> 00:06:45.234
And environmental justice groups
have done just that.

00:06:46.277 --> 00:06:47.445
In a number of ways.

00:06:47.487 --> 00:06:50.615
So we've created
working groups and campaigns

00:06:50.657 --> 00:06:54.202
amongst people who have
a real stake in the outcome,

00:06:54.202 --> 00:06:57.372
those most affected
by harm or new policies.

00:06:57.413 --> 00:07:01.125
And we engage them
in environmental decision making.

00:07:01.167 --> 00:07:04.712
Those are the people
who have the lived experience,

00:07:04.754 --> 00:07:09.300
who can best advise on climate adaptation
and resilience planning.

00:07:09.676 --> 00:07:13.471
Now, if their perspectives
had been known and integrated

00:07:13.513 --> 00:07:18.309
into the inter-agency
emergency response planning

00:07:18.351 --> 00:07:21.688
in New Orleans for Hurricane Katrina,

00:07:21.729 --> 00:07:25.566
then thousands of families would not
have been stranded on their roofs,

00:07:25.608 --> 00:07:27.235
waiting for rescue

00:07:27.276 --> 00:07:29.278
or sleeping in a sports stadium,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:32.031
because the city would have already known

00:07:32.073 --> 00:07:35.159
that lower-income families
living in a flood zone

00:07:35.159 --> 00:07:37.787
did not have a car to evacuate the city

00:07:37.829 --> 00:07:40.790
or a credit card to access a hotel room.

00:07:41.833 --> 00:07:46.421
Another tactic we engage is we engage
community in educational workshops

00:07:46.462 --> 00:07:48.756
that help them better articulate the data,

00:07:48.798 --> 00:07:50.425
testify at hearings,

00:07:50.466 --> 00:07:53.553
train residents to become
citizen scientists

00:07:53.594 --> 00:07:56.764
who collect their own
air and water quality data

00:07:56.806 --> 00:08:00.393
to influence policymakers
and elected officials.

00:08:00.393 --> 00:08:04.522
And those officials
should resource every community

00:08:04.522 --> 00:08:07.567
to develop a climate action plan

00:08:07.567 --> 00:08:11.904
that addresses neighborhood level
response and challenges

00:08:11.904 --> 00:08:14.407
to extreme weather events.

00:08:16.492 --> 00:08:21.289
We've also developed strong,
strong policy agendas at the city,

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:22.540
state and federal levels,

00:08:22.582 --> 00:08:26.210
based on community
perspectives, experience

00:08:26.252 --> 00:08:29.756
and the evolving transition
from a fossil fuel economy.

00:08:30.339 --> 00:08:36.054
And these are tactics that can be applied
within local contexts in any community.

00:08:36.637 --> 00:08:40.641
So as we transition
to renewable energy sources,

00:08:40.641 --> 00:08:45.480
we know that energy insecurity
will impact millions more households

00:08:45.521 --> 00:08:47.690
as utility bills increase.

00:08:47.732 --> 00:08:50.151
And to decarbonize buildings,

00:08:50.193 --> 00:08:54.781
a pre-investment will be needed
to upgrade residential buildings

00:08:54.822 --> 00:08:56.949
in frontline communities

00:08:56.949 --> 00:09:01.370
before electrification
of all buildings will be achieved.

00:09:02.288 --> 00:09:04.707
So I challenge you

00:09:04.749 --> 00:09:06.459
to create the change we need

00:09:06.501 --> 00:09:09.587
by incorporating equity
and lived experience

00:09:09.629 --> 00:09:12.715
into all public and government policies.

00:09:13.424 --> 00:09:16.928
My vision is to redress
the legacy of pollution

00:09:16.928 --> 00:09:19.138
by targeting frontline communities,

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.059
not for pollution,
but to become green zones.

00:09:23.101 --> 00:09:28.898
Green zones where we can incentivize
community shared solar, electrification,

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.485
green businesses, good jobs
and worker cooperatives.

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:35.905
Because today we are
at an inflection point

00:09:35.947 --> 00:09:39.033
with an opportunity
to achieve that vision.

00:09:39.575 --> 00:09:42.161
You know, the Biden administration

00:09:42.161 --> 00:09:44.622
has committed to centering
environmental justice

00:09:44.664 --> 00:09:46.290
in all government policies

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.000
through an executive order

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.295
that directs at least
40 percent of the benefits

00:09:51.337 --> 00:09:54.132
from federal investments in clean energy

00:09:54.132 --> 00:09:56.717
to go to disadvantaged communities.

00:09:56.759 --> 00:09:58.803
So there is an opportunity

00:09:58.845 --> 00:10:03.057
to implement a transformative
and accountable process

00:10:03.099 --> 00:10:07.478
for the fair and just distribution
of benefits of Justice40

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.730
to frontline communities of Black,

00:10:09.772 --> 00:10:11.941
brown and Indigenous peoples.

00:10:12.525 --> 00:10:17.155
But we must make certain
that the investments and the benefits

00:10:17.155 --> 00:10:19.407
reach the communities intended,

00:10:19.448 --> 00:10:23.911
given the bias and ambivalence
of many state and local governments.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:26.164
But if done effectively,

00:10:26.205 --> 00:10:28.332
this can be transformative

00:10:28.374 --> 00:10:33.546
and could stop the cycle
of exploitation and extraction.

00:10:35.006 --> 00:10:36.215
Every community,

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:39.302
every community has the right
to a clean environment.

00:10:39.343 --> 00:10:43.723
And you do not need to be
an environmental champion

00:10:43.723 --> 00:10:47.894
or a climate justice leader
to embrace that value.

00:10:48.603 --> 00:10:51.105
So I call on your humanity

00:10:51.147 --> 00:10:54.859
to center the voices
and perspectives in all policies

00:10:54.901 --> 00:10:58.154
of those most affected
by environmental hazards

00:10:58.196 --> 00:10:59.906
and climate change.

00:10:59.947 --> 00:11:03.868
We can create a legacy
of environmental quality

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:06.078
and climate resilience for all.

00:11:06.078 --> 00:11:08.456
We can do this and we must.

00:11:09.415 --> 00:11:10.583
Thank you.

00:11:10.625 --> 00:11:15.630
(Applause)

